# Batch 7 (2026-10-19): deep time and human origins, Asia, Africa, medieval/early-modern Europe, Americas, Oceania, 20th-century mysteries
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[
# --- part 1: deep time, human origins and prehistory
X('snowball-earth','Snowball Earth: when the planet froze over?',H,'hypothesis',2,-717000000,['geological','palaeomagnetic','geochemical'],
 'A hypothesis that during the Cryogenian Period Earth\'s surface froze nearly entirely, in two major glacial episodes: the Sturtian (c. 717–660 million years ago) and the Marinoan (c. 650–635 million years ago). It explains glacial deposits laid down at tropical palaeolatitudes, banded iron formations and the "cap carbonates" (thick limestone or dolomite layers) that sit sharply on top of the glacial beds. Joseph Kirschvink coined the term in 1992 and proposed the escape mechanism: volcanic CO2 building up until an ultra-greenhouse melted the ice. Paul Hoffman and colleagues revived the idea in Science in 1998 using rocks in Namibia. The episodes came shortly before the Avalon and Cambrian radiations of multicellular life.',
 [('Hard snowball','Proponents argue a fully frozen ocean best explains low-latitude glacial deposits, cap carbonates and isotopic shifts.'),
  ('Slushball Earth','A competing view holds that a thin equatorial band of open (or seasonally open) water remained, which would make it easier for life to survive and for the planet to escape the icehouse.'),
  ('Sceptics','Opponents contest the geological evidence for global glaciation and the physical feasibility of an all-ice ocean; as of 2007 only one "very reliable" (still challenged) data point identified tropical tillites, and some cap carbonates may instead reflect methane release.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.281.5381.1342','wiki:Snowball_Earth'],'Snowball_Earth',end=-635000000,
 dateNote='Sturtian c. 717–660 Ma; Marinoan c. 650–635 Ma',
 changed='1871 onward: ancient glacial deposits found in Scotland, Australia, India and Norway. Douglas Mawson speculated about global glaciation but assumed fixed continents. 1964: W. Brian Harland shows tillites in Svalbard and Greenland formed at tropical latitudes. 1992: Kirschvink coins "snowball Earth". 1998: Hoffman et al. bring it to wide attention. 2010: Macdonald et al. report evidence that the Sturtian glaciation reached sea level near the equator.'),
X('lomekwi','Lomekwi 3: stone tools older than humans',H,'confirmed',2,-3300000,['archaeological','stratigraphic','palaeomagnetic'],
 'In July 2011 a team led by Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis took a wrong turn near Lake Turkana, Kenya, and found stone artefacts at a site they named Lomekwi 3. About 20 excavated artefacts (anvils, cores and flakes, one flake refitting to its core) and about 130 surface finds were dated, from volcanic ash layers and magnetic reversals, to 3.3 million years ago – about 700,000 years older than the Gona tools of Ethiopia and roughly 500,000 years older than the genus Homo. The tools are large (the biggest weighs 15 kg) and were assigned to a new tradition, the "Lomekwian". Published in Nature in May 2015.',
 [('Who made them','Because they predate Homo, the makers may have been Australopithecus or Kenyanthropus (found nearby); it is unclear whether the technology is ancestral to later Homo tools or was forgotten and reinvented.'),
  ('Sceptical view','Some researchers (e.g. Domínguez-Rodrigo and colleagues, 2019) have questioned whether the artefacts were securely in their original layer, and so the 3.3-million-year age; independent researchers who examined the tools were generally supportive of Harmand\'s conclusions.'),
  ('Invisible older tool use','Primate studies suggest a still older, archaeologically almost invisible "organic tool age" of plant and percussive tools may have come first.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature14464','doi:10.1163/21915784-20190006','wiki:Lomekwi'],'Lomekwi'),
X('turkana-boy','Turkana Boy (Nariokotome Boy)',H,'confirmed',3,-1600000,['fossil','archaeological'],
 'KNM-WT 15000, a nearly complete skeleton (108 bones) of a Homo erectus (or Homo ergaster) youth who lived 1.5–1.6 million years ago, found in 1984 by Kamoya Kimeu by the Nariokotome River near Lake Turkana, Kenya. It is the most complete Homo erectus skeleton ever found. He was tall for his age, with a narrow pelvis suited to fully terrestrial walking and a body shape that would shed heat under a hot sun, but kept a low forehead, strong brow ridges and no chin; brain size was about 880 cc.',
 [('How old was he?','Walker and Leakey (1993) estimated 11–12 years from bone maturity; later work assuming a less human-like growth pattern suggests about 8; overall estimates range from 7 to 11, which also changes estimates of his adult height.'),
  ('Speech','Whether his anatomy allowed human-like speech has been debated.')],
 ['doi:10.1007/978-3-662-10382-1','url:https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000|Smithsonian Human Origins Program: KNM-WT 15000|museum','wiki:Turkana_Boy'],None,
 dateNote='1.5–1.6 million years ago'),
X('dmanisi','Dmanisi: the first humans outside Africa?',H,'confirmed',2,-1850000,['fossil','archaeological'],
 'Fossils and stone tools from Dmanisi in Georgia (southern Caucasus) date to 1.85–1.77 million years ago, making them the earliest well-dated hominin fossils in Eurasia. Over a hundred postcranial bones and five well-preserved skulls (Dmanisi Skulls 1–5) show a mosaic: essentially modern legs for long-distance walking, but small bodies (145–166 cm) and small brains (545–775 cc) closer to Homo habilis, with ape-like arms. Their small size challenged the idea that only large-brained, tall Homo erectus could leave Africa.',
 [('One variable species','The Dmanisi team (Lordkipanidze et al., 2013, describing the complete Skull 5) argue all the skulls belong to one population with large age and sex differences, implying early Homo species named from African fossils may be one variable lineage.'),
  ('Several species','Others disagree; a massive jaw (D2600) was made the type of a separate species, Homo georgicus, in 2002, and a 2014 comment in Science disputed the single-species reading. The name is still debated.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.1238484','doi:10.1126/science.1250056','wiki:Dmanisi_hominins'],'Dmanisi_hominins',
 dateNote='1.85–1.77 million years ago'),
X('peking-man','Peking Man and the fossils that vanished in 1941',H,'confirmed',2,-780000,['fossil','archaeological','historical records'],
 'Homo erectus fossils from Zhoukoudian cave, about 50 km south-west of Beijing, appear in deposits from about 800,000 to 230,000 years ago (Shen et al. 2009 used aluminium-26/beryllium-10 burial dating, giving about 770,000 years for the lower layers). The first tooth was found in 1921 and Pei Wenzhong found a skullcap in 1929; Zhoukoudian became the most productive Homo erectus site in the world. During the Japanese occupation, the fossils of at least 40 individuals were packed into two footlockers for shipment to the American Museum of Natural History; after they left Peking Union Medical College on 4 December 1941 they were never seen again. Science survives through Franz Weidenreich\'s casts and descriptions.',
 [('Fate of the fossils (unresolved)','Rumours place them on a sunken ship (such as the Japanese Awa Maru), ground up as traditional medicine, or buried at Qinhuangdao, where a US Marine recalled finding a box of bones in 1947; Japanese and American groups have each been accused of taking them. Large rewards have produced nothing.'),
  ('Out of Asia vs Out of Africa','In the 1920s–30s Peking Man supported a Central Asian origin of humanity and later became a symbol in Chinese national and Marxist narratives; today Out of Africa is the consensus and Peking Man is seen as an offshoot, though some interbreeding with human ancestors is still discussed.'),
  ('Hunters or prey?','Whether Peking Man controlled fire and lived in the cave, or was killed by giant hyenas and dragged there, is debated.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature07741','wiki:Peking_Man'],'Peking_Man',end=-230000,
 dateNote='Deposits c. 800,000–230,000 years ago; fossils lost December 1941'),
X('apollo-11-cave','Apollo 11 Cave stones: Africa\'s oldest figurative art',H,'confirmed',2,-28000,['archaeological','radiocarbon','rock art'],
 'A rock shelter in the ǀAi-ǀAis/Richtersveld park of south-western Namibia (Nama name "Goachanas"), occupied in pulses from about 71,000 to 29,000 years ago. Archaeologist Wolfgang Erich Wendt named it after the Apollo 11 Moon mission, which had just returned to Earth. Seven grey-brown quartzite slabs painted with animals, radiocarbon dated to about 30,000 years BP, are among the oldest figurative art in Africa; the most famous shows a therianthrope combining human hind legs, an antelope body and horns, and a feline head. They are now in the National Museum of Namibia, Windhoek. In 2007 the site was found "severely vandalised".',
 [('Shamanic interpretation','Many rock-art researchers read human–animal figures in southern African art as linked to San trance experience and transformation; whether that applies to images this old is an interpretation, not something the stones themselves can prove.'),
  ('Hunting magic or story','Other readings see the figures as hunting imagery or depictions of myth; the meaning of the composite figure is unknown.')],
 ['doi:10.3213/1612-1651-10170','wiki:Apollo_11_Cave'],'Apollo_11_Cave',
 dateNote='Painted slabs c. 30,000 years BP'),
X('tsodilo-python','Tsodilo Hills and the "python stone" ritual claim',H,'disputed',2,-70000,['archaeological','rock art','oral tradition'],
 'The Tsodilo Hills in north-west Botswana (UNESCO World Heritage, 2001) hold over 4,500 rock paintings at some 500 sites, a few dated to about 24,000 years ago, and are sacred to the San, who regard them as a resting place of the spirits of the dead and as the birthplace of all life. In 2006 Sheila Coulson of the University of Oslo announced that a rock in Rhino Cave resembling a python\'s head, carved with many cup-shaped hollows and associated with 70,000-year-old artefacts, was evidence of the earliest known human ritual.',
 [('Earliest ritual (Coulson)','The python-like rock and the cave\'s images of python, elephant and giraffe – the San\'s three most important animals – were read as a 70,000-year-old ritual site.'),
  ('Critics','Archaeologists who first investigated the site in 1995–96 pointed out that the cupules are not all the same age (some fresh, some heavily patinated), that over 20 nearby rocks have similar hollows without resembling animals, and that the site\'s dating does not support 70,000 years.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1021|UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Tsodilo|heritage body','wiki:Tsodilo'],'Tsodilo',
 dateNote='Claimed c. 70,000 years ago; paintings up to c. 24,000 years old'),
X('altamira','Altamira: cave art dismissed as a forgery',H,'confirmed',2,-36000,['archaeological','uranium-series dating'],
 'In 1879 Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola\'s eight-year-old daughter María led him to the paintings in the Altamira cave in Cantabria, Spain (found in 1868 by tiler Modesto Cubillas). Sautuola and Juan Vilanova y Piera published them in 1880 as Palaeolithic, but leading French prehistorians such as Gabriel de Mortillet and Émile Cartailhac ridiculed the claim at the 1880 congress in Lisbon, partly because prehistoric people were assumed incapable of such art; Sautuola was accused of hiring a contemporary painter. After similar caves were found in France, Cartailhac published "Mea culpa d\'un sceptique" in 1902, 14 years after Sautuola\'s death. Uranium-series dating (Pike et al. 2012) indicates that the earliest paintings there go back about 36,000 years.',
 [('Forgery accusation (historical)','Sceptics pointed to the lack of soot on the walls and the paintings\' freshness; Sautuola later suggested lamps burning marrow fat would have produced little soot.'),
  ('Lesson drawn','The case is often cited as an example of experts rejecting evidence that conflicted with assumptions about "primitive" ancestors; it also shows the opposite risk, since real forgeries exist (see Piltdown).')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.1219957','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/310|UNESCO: Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain|heritage body','wiki:Cave_of_Altamira'],'Cave_of_Altamira',
 dateNote='Earliest paintings c. 36,000 years ago; most famous polychromes much later',
 changed='1880: published as Palaeolithic and widely rejected. 1902: Cartailhac\'s public retraction. 1985: UNESCO World Heritage. 2012: U-series dating pushes the earliest marks back to c. 36,000 years.'),
X('otzi','Ötzi the Iceman',H,'confirmed',2,-3250,['archaeological','forensic','genetic'],
 'The natural mummy of a man who lived around 3275–3230 BCE, found on 19 September 1991 by hikers Helmut and Erika Simon at about 3,210 m near the Tisenjoch pass in the Ötztal Alps on the Austria–Italy border; at first he was taken for a recently dead mountaineer. He carried a copper axe with a yew handle, a flint knife and a quiver of 14 arrows. In 2001 X-rays revealed a flint arrowhead in his left shoulder; he probably bled to death, and he had cuts on his hands and a head injury. A 2023 genome found about 90% Anatolian-farmer ancestry, likely darker skin than present-day Europeans and probable baldness. He is in the South Tyrol Museum of Archaeology, Bolzano.',
 [('Murder on the mountain','Most researchers think he was killed by another person near where he lay; blood traces of several other people on his gear (2003, disputed) led to scenarios of a fight.'),
  ('Burial theory','A 2010 study (Vanzetti et al.) proposed he died lower down and was ceremonially placed on a stone platform higher up; other researchers argue his intact wound and bones show no sign of being moved.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.xgen.2023.100377','url:https://www.iceman.it/en/the-iceman/|South Tyrol Museum of Archaeology: Ötzi the Iceman|museum','wiki:Ötzi'],'Ötzi',
 dateNote='Died c. 3275–3230 BCE; found 1991'),
X('diquis-spheres','The stone spheres of Costa Rica',H,'confirmed',2,600,['archaeological'],
 'More than 300 stone balls on the Diquís Delta and Isla del Caño in southern Costa Rica, attributed to the Diquís culture, ranging from a few centimetres to over 2 metres across and up to 15 tons, mostly carved from gabbro by hammering and sand polishing. They are believed to date from about 600 CE, mostly after 1000 CE and before the Spanish conquest, but dating is hard because most have been moved. Banana-plantation workers of the United Fruit Company uncovered them in the 1930s and, hoping for hidden gold, dynamited some. Doris Stone published the first study in 1943 and Samuel Lothrop surveyed them in 1948. Their settlements became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2014.',
 [('Mainstream meaning','Archaeologists think they were placed in lines along the approach to chiefs\' houses; their exact significance is uncertain, and some suggest they represent the sun and moon.'),
  ('Atlantis and stone-softening (alternative)','Popular myths say they came from Atlantis or were made with a potion that softens rock (geopolymer researcher Joseph Davidovits has been cited for this); most spheres are of acid-resistant gabbro.'),
  ('"Perfectly round"? (claim examined)','Claims of near-perfect roundness came from misreading Lothrop\'s tables, whose three-decimal figures were averages of tape measurements; some spheres vary by over 5 cm in diameter.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1453|UNESCO: Precolumbian Chiefdom Settlements with Stone Spheres of the Diquís|heritage body','wiki:Stone_spheres_of_Costa_Rica'],'Stone_spheres_of_Costa_Rica',
 dateNote='c. 600–1550 CE (uncertain)'),
# --- part 2: Asia
X('delhi-iron-pillar','The iron pillar of Delhi: "the pillar that does not rust"',H,'confirmed',2,400,['archaeological','metallurgical','textual'],
 'A 7.21 m iron pillar (1.12 m below ground), 41 cm in diameter and weighing more than six tonnes, now in the Qutb complex at Mehrauli, Delhi. Its oldest Sanskrit inscription, in Gupta script, records a king "Chandra" – generally identified as Chandragupta II (r. c. 375–415 CE) – raising a standard of Vishnu on a hill called Vishnupada. It was probably first set up elsewhere (possibly outside the Udayagiri Caves) and moved to Delhi by Anangpal Tomar in the 11th century. Its famous resistance to corrosion comes from an even protective layer of crystalline iron hydrogen phosphate hydrate forming on its high-phosphorus iron.',
 [('Ancient astronauts (claim and retraction)','In Chariots of the Gods? (1969) Erich von Däniken cited its lack of corrosion and "unknown" manufacture as evidence of extraterrestrial visitation; told in a 1974 interview that it does rust and that its making is understood, he said he no longer considered it a mystery.'),
  ('Metallurgical view','Materials scientist R. Balasubramaniam called it "a living testimony to the skill of metallurgists of ancient India"; 2009 research found that corrosion has developed evenly over its surface.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/S0010-938X(00)00046-9','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/233|UNESCO: Qutb Minar and its Monuments, Delhi|heritage body','wiki:Iron_pillar_of_Delhi'],'Iron_pillar_of_Delhi',
 dateNote='c. 375–415 CE (Chandragupta II); moved to Delhi in the 11th century'),
X('nine-unknown-men','The Nine Unknown Men of Ashoka',M,'myth-legend',3,-270,['literary','speculative'],
 'A legend of a secret society of nine men guarding nine books of dangerous knowledge. Its best-known form is Talbot Mundy\'s 1923 adventure novel The Nine Unknown, serialised in Adventure magazine, in which the Nine protect the ancient wisdom of Hindu sages. In their 1960 book The Morning of the Magicians, Louis Pauwels and Jacques Bergier claimed the Nine were real, founded by the Mauryan emperor Ashoka around 270 BCE, that Pope Silvester II had met them, and that the 19th-century French writer Louis Jacolliot had insisted on their existence. No historical source for the society is known; it has since inspired many novels and TV series.',
 [('Real secret society (claim)','Pauwels and Bergier presented the Nine as a real, ongoing guardianship of suppressed science; later fiction adds knowledge of time travel and contact with extraterrestrials.'),
  ('Literary origin','Mainstream view: the idea is modern literary and esoteric invention, popularised by a novel and an occult bestseller; no historical source for it is known.')],
 ['wiki:Nine_Unknown_Men','wiki:Ashoka'],'Ashoka',
 dateNote='Claimed founding c. 270 BCE; first known as a story in 1923'),
X('jiroft','Jiroft: a lost Bronze Age civilisation in Iran?',H,'disputed',2,-2500,['archaeological','textual'],
 'An early Bronze Age culture of south-eastern Iran (Kerman and Sistan-Baluchestan), also called the "Intercultural" or Halil Rud style. Before 2001, looters south of Jiroft dug up many carved vessels in the "Intercultural style"; Iranian authorities recovered many, and from 2001 a team led by Yusef Majidzadeh excavated two mounds, Konar Sandal A and B, finding a citadel and over two square kilometres of a city whose heyday was c. 2500–2200 BCE. Majidzadeh proposed an independent civilisation between Elam and the Indus Valley, with its own architecture and language.',
 [('Aratta (Majidzadeh)','Majidzadeh identified Jiroft with Aratta, the rich land of Sumerian epics; many scholars are sceptical, and others (e.g. Daniel Potts, Piotr Steinkeller) connect Konar Sandal with Marhashi, a state east of Elam.'),
  ('Earliest writing? (disputed)','Inscribed bricks and tablets from Konar Sandal, in a geometric script that Majidzadeh related to Elamite writing, were promoted as possibly the oldest writing; a 2007 Science news article asked "Ancient writing or modern fakery?", reflecting doubts about some objects.'),
  ('Criticism','Oscar Muscarella of the Metropolitan Museum criticised sensational announcements and slow scholarly publication, while still acknowledging the site\'s importance.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.317.5838.588','doi:10.1080/05786967.2008.11864738','wiki:Jiroft_culture'],None,end=-2200,
 dateNote='Heyday c. 2500–2200 BCE'),
X('mozu-kofun','Daisen Kofun: Japan\'s giant tomb that may not be opened',H,'confirmed',2,450,['archaeological','textual'],
 'The Daisenryō Kofun in Sakai, Osaka, is the largest of Japan\'s more than 20,000 burial mounds (3rd–7th centuries): a keyhole-shaped tumulus 486 m long, ringed by moats and thought to have been built over about 20 years in the mid-5th century. It is commonly accepted, though it cannot be confirmed, to be the tomb of Emperor Nintoku, and the Imperial Household Agency treats it as such: the mound is off-limits, overgrown and protected as an imperial mausoleum. It belongs to the Mozu-Furuichi Kofun Group, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2019; more than half of the Mozu mounds were destroyed by post-war housing development.',
 [('Who is buried?','The identification with Nintoku follows tradition; because the mound is closed as an imperial mausoleum it cannot be tested by excavation.'),
  ('Open or closed? (two views)','Kofun-period finds show close links with the Korean peninsula (shared armour styles, mirrors from the same moulds, immigrant toraijin clans). Some see closed imperial tombs as limiting inquiry into such questions; others see the closure as proper protection of sacred imperial graves.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1593|UNESCO: Mozu-Furuichi Kofun Group: Mounded Tombs of Ancient Japan|heritage body','wiki:Mozu_Kofun_Group','wiki:Kofun_period'],'Mozu_Kofun_Group',
 dateNote='Mid-5th century CE'),
X('urashima-taro','Urashima Tarō: the fisherman who lost 300 years',M,'myth-legend',2,478,['textual','folklore'],
 'A Japanese tale in which a fisherman rescues a turtle and is taken to the palace of the sea-god (in older versions, the land of immortals, Hōrai or Tokoyo). He stays what seems a few days; when he goes home, centuries have passed, and when he opens the forbidden box (tamatebako) the princess gave him, he becomes an old man. It goes back to 8th-century texts: the Tango Province Fudoki, the Man\'yōshū and the Nihon Shoki, which places Urashimako\'s departure in the 22nd year of Emperor Yūryaku (478 CE in Aston\'s translation). A document at Urashima Jinja shrine says he returned in 825 CE.',
 [('Folklore type','Folklorists class it with tales of "the relativity of time" (ATU 681), found worldwide, including the Chinese woodcutter Wang Zhi who watches immortals play a game while years pass ("Rotten Axe Handle Mountain"); the Chinese story is sometimes conjectured as a source.'),
  ('Modern readings (speculative)','The motif has been compared with relativistic time dilation (sometimes called the "Urashima effect" in Japan) and with "missing time" in modern abduction narratives; such parallels are modern interpretations, not part of the tale\'s origin.')],
 ['wiki:Urashima_Tarō'],'Urashima_Tarō',
 dateNote='Nihon Shoki dates the voyage to 478 CE; texts from the 8th century'),
X('kakudmi','King Kakudmi: an audience with Brahma while ages pass',M,'myth-legend',3,500,['textual','religious text'],
 'In Hindu texts (the Mahabharata, Harivamsha, Vishnu Purana, Devi Bhagavatam and Bhagavata Purana), King Kakudmi (Raivata) of Kushasthali takes his daughter Revati to Brahma\'s realm to ask which suitor she should marry. They wait while Brahma listens to a musical performance; Brahma then explains that many ages have passed on Earth, and all the suitors and their descendants are long dead. Returning, they find that 27 chaturyugas (about 117 million years) have passed, their city Kushasthali has become Dvaraka, and men have "dwindled in stature"; Revati marries Balarama, brother of Krishna.',
 [('Religious meaning','Within Hindu cosmology the story illustrates the vast, nested cycles of time (yugas and manvantaras) and the difference between divine and human time.'),
  ('Cross-cultural pattern','It is one of the oldest stories of time passing differently in another realm, alongside Urashima Tarō (Japan) and Oisín in Tír na nÓg (Ireland); writers have compared it with relativistic time dilation, a modern analogy rather than an ancient claim.')],
 ['wiki:Kakudmi','wiki:Revati'],'Revati',
 dateNote='Undated legend recorded in the Mahabharata, Harivamsha and Puranas'),
X('kaguya-hime','Kaguya-hime: the princess from the Moon',M,'myth-legend',2,900,['textual','folklore'],
 'The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter (Taketori Monogatari), by an unknown author of the late 9th or early 10th century, is considered the oldest surviving Japanese monogatari. A bamboo cutter finds a tiny girl in a glowing bamboo stalk; she grows into the beautiful Kaguya-hime, sets impossible tasks for five suitors and refuses the emperor. Finally she reveals she is from the Moon; an embassy of heavenly beings descends, the emperor\'s guards are blinded by a strange light, and she returns to the Moon. The emperor has her elixir of immortality burned on the mountain closest to heaven – one legend of the name of Mount Fuji (fushi, "immortality"). The oldest surviving manuscript dates from 1592.',
 [('Literary and religious roots','Scholars relate it to Chinese tales such as Chang\'e, who fled to the Moon with the elixir of immortality, and to Daoist and Buddhist ideas of immortals and celestial realms.'),
  ('Visitor from the sky (modern reading, speculative)','Some modern readers describe the descending heavenly host and blinding light as resembling a UFO encounter and Kaguya as an extraterrestrial; this is a modern interpretation of a court romance.')],
 ['wiki:The_Tale_of_the_Bamboo_Cutter'],'The_Tale_of_the_Bamboo_Cutter',
 dateNote='Composed late 9th or early 10th century'),
X('kailasa','Kailasa temple, Ellora: a temple carved from the top down',H,'confirmed',2,760,['archaeological','epigraphic'],
 'The largest of the 34 Hindu, Buddhist and Jain rock-cut temples and monasteries at Ellora, Maharashtra (UNESCO World Heritage). The Kailasa temple (Cave 16) is a monolith cut out of the basalt cliff, carved vertically from the top of the rock downward; its sanctuary rises 32.6 m above the court. It has no dedicatory inscription, but copper-plate grants of c. 812–813 CE credit "Krishnaraja", generally identified as the Rashtrakuta king Krishna I (r. c. 756–773), with a temple so wondrous that "even the gods and the architect were astonished".',
 [('Legend of a temple in a week','A medieval Marathi legend (Katha-Kalpataru, c. 1470–1535) says a queen vowed to fast until she saw a temple\'s top; the architect Kokasa finished the shikhara in a week by carving from the top. M. K. Dhavalikar suggests Kokasa may really have been the chief architect.'),
  ('How long did it take?','Dhavalikar estimated that 250 labourers cutting about 4 cubic feet each per day could finish it in about 5.5 years, and that quarrying a monolith is less work than hauling stone; others think its mixed styles show work over several reigns.'),
  ('Lost-technology claims (speculative)','Popular alternative-history media sometimes claim such a monolith required lost advanced technology; archaeologists see no need for this, pointing to the inscriptions, labour estimates and comparable temples such as the Virupaksha at Pattadakal.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/243|UNESCO: Ellora Caves|heritage body','wiki:Kailasa_temple,_Ellora'],'Kailasa_temple,_Ellora',
 dateNote='Mainly 8th century CE (Krishna I, r. c. 756–773)'),
X('shambhala','Shambhala, the hidden kingdom',M,'myth-legend',2,1050,['religious text','esoteric','speculative'],
 'A spiritual kingdom in Tibetan Buddhist tradition, described in the Kalachakra ("Wheel of Time") tantra, which reached Tibet in the 11th century; the Bon religion has a similar land, Olmo Lung Ring. The name comes from a city in the Hindu Puranas, where the Vishnu Purana prophesies Shambhala as the birthplace of Kalki, who will begin a new golden age. The Kalachakra prophesies that when the world sinks into war and greed, the 25th Kalki king will ride out of Shambhala with a great army, in a final battle dated to 2424 or 2425, followed by a golden age.',
 [('Spiritual reading','Some Buddhist teachers interpret Shambhala as a pure land or state of mind reached through practice rather than a place on a map.'),
  ('Physical hidden land (searches)','Nicholas and Helena Roerich led expeditions (1924–28, 1934–35), believing Belukha Mountain in the Altai to be an entrance; in the 1920s Soviet secret-police cryptographer Gleb Bokii and Alexander Barchenko planned an expedition to retrieve its wisdom. Alexandra David-Néel linked it with Balkh in Afghanistan.'),
  ('Esoteric versions','Theosophical and neo-Theosophical writers such as Alice Bailey made it the seat of a hidden brotherhood of masters guiding humanity; it influenced the fictional Shangri-La.')],
 ['wiki:Shambhala','wiki:Kalachakra'],'Kalachakra',
 dateNote='Kalachakra tradition from the 11th century; prophesied battle in 2424/2425'),
X('yeti','The Yeti ("Abominable Snowman")',M,'myth-legend',2,1921,['folklore','eyewitness','genetic'],
 'A large, hairy, ape-like creature said to live in the Himalayas. The English name "Abominable Snowman" dates from the 1921 British Everest reconnaissance led by Charles Howard-Bury, who reported footprints at about 6,400 m. Interest peaked after Eric Shipton photographed large prints in 1951 and Hillary and Tenzing reported footprints in 1953. Monastery "Yeti scalps" (Pangboche, Khumjung) were examined by Hillary\'s 1960–61 expedition. DNA studies have repeatedly matched alleged Yeti hair, bone and scat to bears.',
 [('Unknown primate (cryptozoology)','Believers point to consistent Sherpa and Tibetan accounts (several named kinds, such as migoi, "wild man"), footprint casts and photographs as evidence of an undiscovered ape.'),
  ('Bears (genetic studies)','Sykes et al. (2014) matched two Himalayan samples to an ancient polar-bear sequence and suggested a hybrid bear; later work (2015) found brown bear more likely, and Lan et al. (2017) identified most "Yeti" samples as Himalayan or Tibetan brown bears or Asian black bear, and one as a domestic dog. Daniel C. Taylor (2017) argues the Asian black bear explains classic footprints.'),
  ('Folklore view','Folklorists trace the Yeti to Sherpa folklore plus misidentified animals; the Tibetan root word appears to mean "rock bear".')],
 ['doi:10.1098/rspb.2014.0161','doi:10.1098/rspb.2017.1804','wiki:Yeti'],'Yeti',
 dateNote='Folklore older; Western name from 1921'),
X('death-worm','The Mongolian death worm (olgoi-khorkhoi)',M,'myth-legend',3,1926,['folklore','eyewitness'],
 'A cryptid of Mongolian folklore said to live in the Gobi Desert: sausage-shaped, about two feet long, headless and legless, and so poisonous that touching it means instant death, according to a description by Mongolian prime minister Damdinbazar in 1922. It reached the West through Roy Chapman Andrews\'s 1926 book On the Trail of Ancient Man; Andrews himself did not believe in it. Later tales say it can kill at a distance with venom or electric shocks. In 1983, locals who claimed to have seen it identified a Tartar sand boa as the same animal.',
 [('Undiscovered animal (cryptozoology)','Searchers, from Ivan Mackerle (1990, 1992, using a motorised "thumper" inspired by Dune) to Richard Freeman (2005) and TV teams, found nothing; Freeman concluded the powers were apocryphal but that sightings might involve an unknown worm lizard.'),
  ('Folklore and known animals','Mainstream view: a legend built on misidentified real animals such as the sand boa, amplified by retelling; palaeontologist Ivan Yefremov heard the legend many times in the 1940s but met no one who had seen it.')],
 ['wiki:Mongolian_death_worm'],'Mongolian_death_worm',
 dateNote='Described 1922; publicised 1926'),
X('baigong-pipes','The Baigong "pipes" of Qinghai',H,'debunked',3,2002,['geological','media reports'],
 'Pipe-like structures in and around White Mountain (Baigongshan), about 40 km south-west of Delingha in Qinghai, China, found in and near a cave and along the shore of Lake Toson. They became famous through Chinese newspaper reports in June 2002, which described "rusty" iron pipes; a local smeltery analysis reported about 30% iron oxide with much silica and calcium oxide. A 2003 report said Chinese scientists had found organic plant material and tree rings in them, so they were judged to be fossilised trees or roots; the local government promotes them as a tourist attraction.',
 [('Artificial pipes (popular claim)','Media and alternative writers presented them as ancient or non-human iron plumbing, sometimes linked to an extraterrestrial base; this view relies on the pipe-like shape and iron content.'),
  ('Natural formations','Mainstream explanation: iron-rich concretions formed around fossil tree roots; the oxide analysis is consistent with natural minerals such as calcite and iron oxides.')],
 ['wiki:Baigong_Pipes','url:https://skeptoid.com/episodes/4181|Skeptoid #181: The Baigong Pipes (Brian Dunning, 2009)|podcast'],None),
# --- part 3: Africa, medieval and early-modern Europe, the Atlantic
X('library-alexandria','The Library of Alexandria: burned once, or faded away?',H,'confirmed',1,-285,['textual','historical records'],
 'Part of the Mouseion research institution in Alexandria, Egypt, probably built under Ptolemy II Philadelphus after plans attributed to Demetrius of Phalerum and Ptolemy I. The Ptolemies aggressively acquired papyrus scrolls (estimates range from 40,000 to 700,000), and scholars there included Callimachus (whose Pinakes is sometimes called the first library catalogue), Eratosthenes (who estimated Earth\'s circumference) and Hero (who described a steam engine). Its decline was gradual: a purge of intellectuals in 145 BCE under Ptolemy VIII, a fire during Julius Caesar\'s siege in 48 BCE (extent unclear; Strabo still visited the Mouseion c. 20 BCE), dwindling Roman support, and Aurelian\'s destruction of its quarter around 272 CE. The daughter library in the Serapeum was demolished in 391 CE under Bishop Theophilus.',
 [('Single catastrophic burning (popular view)','Popular culture often imagines one great fire destroying all ancient knowledge, blamed on Caesar, Christians or the Arab conquest.'),
  ('Arab-conquest story (disputed)','Later Arabic sources, the earliest being al-Qifti centuries afterwards, say Caliph Omar ordered the books burned after the conquest of 642 CE; since Eusèbe Renaudot (1713) most scholars have been sceptical because of the time gap and the authors\' political motives.'),
  ('Lost-knowledge speculation','Some alternative writers suggest the library held records of lost civilisations or advanced science; there is no inventory to test this, and much classical literature survived through copies elsewhere.')],
 ['wiki:Library_of_Alexandria'],'Library_of_Alexandria',end=391,
 dateNote='Founded early 3rd century BCE; gradual decline to the 3rd–4th centuries CE'),
X('wagadu-bida','Wagadu and the serpent Bida',M,'myth-legend',2,300,['oral tradition','textual','archaeological'],
 'The Ghana Empire (Wagadu in Soninke), in what is now south-eastern Mauritania and western Mali, was described by Arab writers from the 9th century onward and fell to the Sosso around 1203. Soninke oral traditions say the ancestor Dinga came "from the east" and had to kill a serpent deity, Bida, or make a pact with it: one maiden sacrificed each year in return for rainfall (and in some versions a constant supply of gold). Wagadu fell when a noble killed Bida to save a maiden (against her wish), unleashing a curse of seven years of drought and famine; some versions add that the gold moved to Bure. Archaeology at Dhar Tichitt shows complex ancestral Soninke settlements by about 1600 BCE.',
 [('Memory of real change (interpretation)','Pythons are associated with water and the seasonal rains, and snake deities are prominent in West African religions; one reading is that the drought and the moving gold echo real Sahelian drying and the shift of gold production from Bambuk to Bure.'),
  ('Foreign-origin theories (rejected)','Some 16th–17th-century chronicles claimed Ghana\'s rulers were "white" Berbers; modern scholars generally reject this, and earlier Arab accounts describe Ghana\'s rulers as Black.'),
  ('Myth as charter','The serpent pact can be read as a charter legitimising the ruling clans and the sacred bond between king, rain and land, a pattern found in other sacred-kingship traditions.')],
 ['wiki:Ghana_Empire'],'Ghana_Empire',
 dateNote='Wagadu rose c. 3rd century CE; fell c. 1203'),
X('gedi','Gedi: the abandoned Swahili town in the forest',H,'confirmed',2,1050,['archaeological'],
 'A walled medieval Swahili stone town in the Arabuko-Sokoke Forest near the coast of Kenya, occupied from as early as the 11th century and abandoned by the mid-17th century (UNESCO World Heritage: the Historic Town and Archaeological Site of Gedi). It has mosques, a palace, stone houses, grid streets, storm-water sumps and lavatories; finds include Chinese porcelain and coins, Islamic glazed wares, Venetian beads and cowries. Its prosperity peaked in the 15th–16th centuries. Excavated by James Kirkman from 1948. Local Giriama (Mijikenda) people regard it as sacred and say it is guarded by the spirits of its priests, the "Old Ones", who curse anyone who harms it.',
 [('Why abandoned?','Explanations include Portuguese attempts to monopolise trade and armed intervention, a falling water table (the well by the Great Mosque was deepened), a Wazimba raid along the coast in 1589, and Oromo migrations and raids.'),
  ('Spirit-guarded ruin (local tradition)','For many Giriama the site is still inhabited by ancestral and evil spirits; respecting it is a religious obligation rather than a mystery to be solved.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1720|UNESCO: The Historic Town and Archaeological Site of Gedi|heritage body','wiki:Ruins_of_Gedi'],'Ruins_of_Gedi',end=1650,
 dateNote='11th century to mid-17th century'),
X('inkanyamba','Inkanyamba, the storm serpent of Howick Falls',M,'myth-legend',3,1962,['oral tradition','eyewitness'],
 'In Zulu and Xhosa lore of South Africa, the inkanyamba (Xhosa: "snake cloud" or "snake in the sky", i.e. tornado) is a huge serpent whose coils reach from heaven to earth, the cause of tornadoes and a storm, water and hail power. It is said to live in rivers and pools of KwaZulu-Natal, above all the pool below Howick Falls on the Umgeni River ("Howie"), and around Hogsback in the Eastern Cape. Its anger brings summer storms; appeasing it brings rain and fertility. Shiny corrugated-iron roofs are said to attract it, mistaking them for water. A game ranger reported a horse-headed serpent on the Umgeni in 1962, with further local sightings in 1974, 1981 and 1995.',
 [('Living creature (cryptozoology)','Some treat the sightings as an unknown large eel or serpent.'),
  ('Misidentification or hoax','Commentators suggest Nile crocodiles, large eels or hoaxes to promote tourism.'),
  ('Weather spirit','In traditional belief it is better understood as the spirit of the storm itself, comparable to other African and Australian rain serpents.')],
 ['wiki:Inkanyamba'],'Howick_Falls',
 dateNote='Traditional belief; modern sightings 1962–1995'),
X('lake-nyos','Lake Nyos: the lake that exploded',H,'confirmed',2,1986,['scientific','oral tradition'],
 'On the night of 21 August 1986 Lake Nyos, a crater lake in north-western Cameroon, released some 100,000–300,000 tons of carbon dioxide in a limnic eruption. The heavier-than-air cloud flowed down the valleys and suffocated 1,746 people and 3,500 livestock up to about 25 km away. Two years earlier a similar event had occurred at Lake Monoun; these are the only two recorded limnic eruptions in modern history. Degassing pipes were installed from 2001, and by 2019 the lake had reached a near steady state.',
 [('Legends of "bad lakes"','Anthropologist Eugenia Shanklin documented that stories of misbehaving or exploding lakes had circulated in the Cameroon Grassfields for decades before 1986, collected over 80 years by ethnographers, administrators and missionaries; some read them as memories of earlier gas releases.'),
  ('What triggered it?','Most geologists suspect a landslide; others propose a small volcanic eruption on the lake bed or cold rainwater; some argue no trigger was needed because the CO2 layering is inherently unstable.'),
  ('Biblical analogy (speculative)','The 2006 documentary The Exodus Decoded used Lake Nyos to suggest a natural explanation for some biblical plagues.')],
 ['doi:10.1144/GSL.SP.2007.273.01.14','wiki:Lake_Nyos_disaster'],'Lake_Nyos_disaster',
 dateNote='21 August 1986'),
X('black-death','The Black Death',H,'confirmed',1,1346,['textual','genetic','archaeological'],
 'A plague pandemic that struck Europe, the Middle East and North Africa in 1346–1353, killing up to 50 million people: around 30–60% of Europe\'s population and about a third of the Middle East\'s. It was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis, confirmed by ancient-DNA studies of victims (Bos et al. 2011), and in 2022 genomes from 1338–39 graves near Lake Issyk-Kul in Kyrgyzstan, whose tombstones name "pestilence", pointed to its source in central Eurasia. It reached Europe via Genoese ships from Kaffa in Crimea in 1347. Europe\'s population did not recover its 14th-century level until the 16th century.',
 [('Contemporary explanations','The Paris Medical Faculty blamed a conjunction of planets causing "a great pestilence in the air"; many saw divine punishment, prompting flagellant movements; others blamed Jews, foreigners, lepers and beggars. In the Strasbourg massacre of February 1349 about 2,000 Jews were murdered.'),
  ('How it spread (debated)','The classic model is rat-flea-human; a 2018 model argued spread by human fleas and body lice fits death records better, and archaeologist Barney Sloane argued there is too little evidence of rat die-offs in medieval London.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature10549','doi:10.1038/s41586-022-04800-3','wiki:Black_Death'],'Black_Death',end=1353,
 dateNote='1346–1353 in Europe'),
X('green-children','The Green Children of Woolpit',M,'myth-legend',2,1150,['textual (chronicle)','folklore'],
 'Two children with greenish skin reportedly appeared one summer in the 12th century (possibly under King Stephen, 1135–54) at Woolpit, Suffolk. They spoke an unknown language and would eat only raw broad beans; in time they ate other food and lost their colour, but the boy died. The girl, after learning English, said they came from a land where the sun never shone and the light was like twilight, called St Martin\'s Land (one version adds that everything there was green). The only near-contemporary accounts are by William of Newburgh (c. 1189) and Ralph of Coggeshall (1220s), who drew on Sir Richard de Calne.',
 [('Lost Flemish children (Harris, 1998)','Paul Harris proposed they were orphans of persecuted Flemish immigrants from Fornham St Martin, perhaps after the Battle of Fornham (1173), green from malnutrition (chlorosis); John Clark questioned his use of the evidence.'),
  ('Otherworld or extraterrestrials','Others see a folktale of visitors from another world, underground or extraterrestrial; Robert Burton (1621) said they "fell from Heaven", an idea used in Francis Godwin\'s early science fiction The Man in the Moone (1638).'),
  ('Ritual or literature','Folklorist Martin Walsh reads it as a garbled harvest (Martinmas) ritual; some literary scholars see it as a chronicler\'s reflection on outsiders and identity; William Camden (1586) thought it a hoax.')],
 ['wiki:Green_children_of_Woolpit'],'Green_children_of_Woolpit',
 dateNote='12th century (c. 1135–1154?)'),
X('dancing-plague','The dancing plague of 1518',H,'confirmed',2,1518,['textual','historical records'],
 'From July to September 1518 between about 50 and 400 people in Strasbourg danced uncontrollably for days or weeks. It began on 14 July when a woman, Frau Troffea, started dancing in the street; within days more than thirty joined her. Physician notes, cathedral sermons, chronicles and city-council records agree that people danced; they do not say why. Dancers collapsed from exhaustion and some may have died, though the number of deaths is disputed and some claims (up to 15 a day) come from later accounts. The outbreak subsided in early September.',
 [('Stress-induced mass psychogenic illness (Waller)','Historian John Waller argues that famine, disease and fear of supernatural punishment (such as St Vitus\'s curse) put people into an altered state in which dancing spread; it is the most popular explanation.'),
  ('Ergot poisoning','Some propose ergotism from fungus-infected rye; Waller replies that ergot victims could hardly dance for days and would not all react the same way.'),
  ('Divine punishment (contemporary view)','Many in Strasbourg feared a punishment from Saint Vitus, believed to compel sinners to dance. Physicians prescribed dancing it out, with guild halls and musicians reportedly provided; when that backfired the council banned public dancing and music and sent dancers to the shrine of St Vitus with red shoes, crosses and holy water. Earlier dancing manias (St Vitus\'s and St John\'s dances) occurred in medieval central Europe.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(09)60386-X','wiki:Dancing_plague_of_1518'],'Dancing_plague_of_1518',
 dateNote='July–September 1518'),
X('salem','The Salem witch trials',H,'confirmed',2,1692,['historical records','court records'],
 'Between February 1692 and May 1693 more than 200 people in colonial Massachusetts were accused of witchcraft, starting in Salem Village (now Danvers) when girls including Betty Parris and Abigail Williams showed fits. Thirty were found guilty and nineteen hanged; Giles Corey died under torture for refusing to plead, and at least five died in jail. Much evidence was "spectral evidence" – testimony that the accused\'s apparition tormented the afflicted. The trials ended when leading clergy doubted that evidence. Several accusers later admitted fabricating charges; the convictions were annulled in 1711, more victims were exonerated in 1957 and 2001, and the last, Elizabeth Johnson Jr., in 2022.',
 [('Mass hysteria and social conflict','Historians link the panic to stress from frontier wars with Native Americans, village factional feuds (e.g. the Putnam family), and jealousy, spite and need for attention.'),
  ('Ergot poisoning','Linnda Caporael (1976) proposed convulsive ergotism from infected rye bread; critics argue the symptoms and pattern do not fit well.'),
  ('Other medical theories','Researchers have also suggested bird-borne encephalitis lethargica and sleep paralysis for the night-time "attacks".'),
  ('Real witchcraft (contemporary view)','Most people at the time, including the judges, believed the Devil was really at work; some ministers such as Increase Mather urged caution about spectral evidence.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.769159','wiki:Salem_witch_trials'],'Salem_witch_trials',end=1693,
 dateNote='February 1692 – May 1693'),
X('oisin','Oisín in Tír na nÓg: three years that were three hundred',M,'myth-legend',3,300,['textual','folklore'],
 'In Irish myth (the Fenian Cycle), Oisín, poet son of Fionn mac Cumhaill, is taken by the fairy woman Niamh of the Golden Hair to Tír na nÓg, "the land of the young". After what seems three years he wants to see his comrades, the Fianna. Niamh lends him her white horse and warns him not to touch the ground. In Ireland he finds that 300 years have passed; leaning to help men move a stone, he falls from the horse and instantly becomes a withered old man. In some versions he tells the stories of the Fianna to Saint Patrick before he dies.',
 [('Christian frame','The meeting with Saint Patrick (as in the Acallam na Senórach) sets the old heroes against the new faith; versions differ on whether Oisín defends druidism or converts.'),
  ('Cross-cultural motif','It closely parallels Urashima Tarō (Japan) and King Kakudmi (India): time runs differently in the otherworld and the return home is catastrophic; folklorists class such tales together (ATU 681 and related).'),
  ('Modern readings (speculative)','Some modern writers liken such stories to time dilation or to "missing time" in UFO narratives.')],
 ['wiki:Oisín','wiki:Tír_na_nÓg'],'Tír_na_nÓg',
 dateNote='Fenian Cycle setting c. 3rd century CE; surviving texts medieval'),
X('mary-celeste','The Mary Celeste: the ship found empty',H,'confirmed',2,1872,['historical records','court records'],
 'On 4 December 1872 the Canadian brigantine Dei Gratia found the American-registered Mary Celeste adrift and deserted between the Azores and Portugal, seaworthy, under partial sail, with her lifeboat missing. She had left New York for Genoa on 7 November with Captain Benjamin Briggs, his wife and infant daughter and seven crew, and a cargo of 1,701 barrels of alcohol. The last log entry (25 November) put her off Santa Maria in the Azores. Cargo, provisions and personal belongings were intact; no one aboard was ever seen again. The Gibraltar salvage hearings considered mutiny, piracy and insurance fraud but found no convincing evidence.',
 [('Abandoned in fear','A widely discussed explanation is that the crew took to the lifeboat as a temporary precaution (e.g. fearing the alcohol cargo or a leak) and lost the ship; Briggs\'s cousin Oliver Cobb suggested such a temporary transfer.'),
  ('Other natural hypotheses','Alcohol fumes from the cargo, a seaquake, waterspouts, or attack by a giant squid.'),
  ('Paranormal and fiction','Arthur Conan Doyle\'s 1884 story "J. Habakuk Jephson\'s Statement" added false details (and the "Marie Celeste" spelling); paranormal explanations followed. In 1885 a later captain deliberately wrecked her off Haiti in an insurance fraud.')],
 ['wiki:Mary_Celeste'],'Mary_Celeste',
 dateNote='Found 4 December 1872'),
# --- part 4: Oceania, the Americas and 20th/21st-century mysteries
X('hawaiki','Hawaiki, the Polynesian homeland and land of the dead',M,'myth-legend',2,1280,['oral tradition','linguistic','genetic','archaeological'],
 'In Polynesian tradition Hawaiki (ʻAvaiki in the Cook Islands, Savaiʻi in Samoa, Havaiʻi in Tahiti, Hawaiʻi in Hawaiʻi) is the original homeland from which ancestors sailed across the Pacific in open canoes; Māori trace their ancestry to groups arriving in Aotearoa in about 40 named waka. It is also the underworld to which spirits return after death – in New Zealand via Cape Reinga (Te Rerenga Wairua) at the northern tip. Linguists reconstruct the word as Proto-Nuclear Polynesian *sawaiki. Anne Salmond states that Havaiʻi is the old name of Raiatea; when Cook reached New Zealand in 1769, the Raiatean navigator Tupaia discussed Hawaiki and Rangiatea with Māori priests.',
 [('A real island (Raiatea and the Society Islands)','Many scholars identify the Māori Hawaiki with Raiatea (Havaiʻi) in the Society Islands; DNA, linguistic, botanical and archaeological evidence trace Polynesians ultimately to island East Asia, possibly Taiwan.'),
  ('Ancestors rather than a place','Because cognates mean "spirits" (Samoan saualiʻi) and "chiefs" (Tongan houʻeiki), some scholars think Hawaiki originally meant chiefly ancestors and the social order rather than one location.'),
  ('Accidental drift vs deliberate voyaging','Until the early 21st century many anthropologists thought settlement happened by accidental drift; revived double-hulled canoes sailing with traditional navigation (Polynesian Voyaging Society) have shown deliberate voyages were feasible. Thor Heyerdahl\'s 1947 Kon-Tiki raft argued for settlement from South America, which genetic evidence does not support, although sweet potatoes show some contact with the Americas.')],
 ['wiki:Hawaiki','wiki:Polynesian_navigation'],'Polynesian_navigation',
 dateNote='Undated ancestral tradition; Māori arrival in Aotearoa c. late 13th century'),
X('min-min','The Min Min lights of outback Australia',H,'disputed',2,1918,['oral tradition','eyewitness'],
 'Fuzzy, disc-shaped lights reported hovering just above the horizon in outback Australia, mostly in the Channel Country of western Queensland (Boulia), but also from Brewarrina in New South Wales to the Pilbara (reported by the Ngarluma people). Stories about them exist in several Aboriginal cultures from before colonisation; a stockman\'s sighting at the Min Min Hotel in 1918 is often cited, and a possible 1838 account may describe something else. In folklore the lights follow or approach people, vanish when shot at, keep pace with moving cars, and anyone who chases and catches them never returns.',
 [('Fata Morgana mirage (Pettigrew)','Scientist Jack Pettigrew proposed that a temperature inversion refracts distant lights from beyond the horizon – campfires in early accounts (stationary lights), car headlights later (moving lights).'),
  ('Bioluminescence or geophysics','Pettigrew also floated glowing insects or owls, and piezoelectric or marsh-gas effects; no such animal has been found, and the lights are seen where the geology does not suit those effects.'),
  ('Spirits or unknown phenomenon','In Aboriginal traditions the lights are bound up with spirits and country; in settler folklore they are an eerie, unknowable presence. Similar "ghost lights" are reported worldwide (Hessdalen, Marfa, the Naga fireballs).')],
 ['wiki:Min_Min_light'],'Min_Min_light',
 dateNote='Aboriginal traditions predate colonisation; Min Min Hotel sighting 1918'),
X('yowie','The Yowie, Australia\'s "hairy man"',M,'myth-legend',3,1875,['oral tradition','eyewitness'],
 'An ape-like, hairy, bipedal creature of Australian folklore, 2.1–3.6 m tall, with origins in Aboriginal oral history, particularly in the eastern states, where it has many names (Quinkin in parts of Queensland; Ghindaring, Jurrawarra, Doolaga and others in New South Wales). Rev. William Ridley recorded "Yō-wī" among the Gamilaraay in 1875 as a spirit that roams at night. Nineteenth-century settlers wrote of the "Yahoo" or "hairy man of the wood"; newspaper reports of "indigenous apes" followed. A AU$200,000 reward offered in the mid-1970s for a captured Yowie was never claimed.',
 [('Relict ape or early human (cryptozoology)','Self-described cryptozoologist Rex Gilroy, who claims over 3,000 reports since the mid-1970s, argues for a surviving population of extinct apes or early Homo.'),
  ('Folklore and misreading','Historian Graham Joyner (1977) documented 19th-century "yahoo" reports and argues the modern Yowie barely existed before the mid-1970s and grew from conflating those accounts; footprint reports are inconsistent in shape and toe number.'),
  ('Aboriginal tradition','Some Aboriginal accounts describe the hairy beings as an ancient people or as spirits, a meaning distinct from the zoological "ape" framing.')],
 ['wiki:Yowie'],'Yowie',
 dateNote='"Yō-wī" recorded 1875; modern wave from the 1970s'),
X('wendigo','The Wendigo: the spirit of hunger',M,'myth-legend',2,1661,['oral tradition','historical records','psychiatric'],
 'In the beliefs of Algonquian-speaking peoples (Ojibwe, Saulteaux, Cree, Naskapi, Innu) the wendigo is a malevolent cannibal spirit linked with the north, winter, cold and famine: gaunt to emaciation, ash-grey, sometimes a giant that grows as it eats, with a heart of ice. It can possess people, giving them insatiable hunger for human flesh; people who broke the cannibalism taboo could become wendigos. The Jesuit Relations described such cases in 1661. In the winter of 1878 Plains Cree trapper Swift Runner killed and ate his family although emergency food supplies were within reach, and was later executed; in 1907 Oji-Cree leader Jack Fiddler, famed for defeating wendigos, was arrested for killing people believed to be turning wendigo.',
 [('Wendigo psychosis (culture-bound syndrome)','Psychiatrists described "wendigo psychosis", an intense craving for human flesh and fear of becoming a cannibal; in the 1980s researchers debated whether it was a real syndrome or a fabrication of credulous anthropologists, with others citing credible eyewitness accounts.'),
  ('Moral and social meaning','Many First Nations writers treat the wendigo as the embodiment of greed and selfishness; some communities see insatiable greed and environmental destruction as wendigo symptoms. A satirical famine dance (wiindigookaanzhimowin) reinforced the taboo.'),
  ('Literary monster','Algernon Blackwood\'s 1910 novella The Wendigo popularised the word in English horror fiction.')],
 ['wiki:Wendigo'],'Wendigo',
 dateNote='Traditional belief; recorded from 1661'),
X('foo-fighters','Foo fighters: the lights that chased WWII pilots',H,'disputed',2,1944,['eyewitness','military records'],
 'Allied aircrews in the Second World War reported glowing balls and lights that seemed to follow their aircraft over Europe and the Pacific. RAF crews reported lights following them from as early as March 1942; the name came from the US 415th Night Fighter Squadron in November 1944, after radar operator Donald J. Meiers borrowed "foo" from the Smokey Stover comic strip ("where there\'s foo, there\'s fire"). On 13 December 1944 Allied headquarters in Paris described the phenomenon in a press release as a "new German weapon". In the Pacific, crews described a large burning sphere that "just hung in the sky".',
 [('Enemy secret weapon (wartime view)','Witnesses presumed they were German or Japanese devices; one wartime intelligence assessment suggested some were Me 262 jets or flak rockets while admitting the evidence was "sketchy".'),
  ('Natural or optical phenomena','The 1953 Robertson Panel suggested electrostatic effects like St Elmo\'s fire, electromagnetic phenomena, or light reflecting from ice crystals; ball lightning has also been proposed.'),
  ('Early UAP (ufology)','Many ufologists see foo fighters as among the first military UAP encounters, a precursor of the 1947 "flying saucer" wave.')],
 ['wiki:Foo_fighter'],'Foo_fighter',
 dateNote='Reported 1942–1945; named November 1944'),
X('dyatlov','The Dyatlov Pass incident',H,'disputed',2,1959,['forensic','historical records','modelling'],
 'On the night of 1–2 February 1959 nine experienced ski hikers from the Ural Polytechnical Institute, led by Igor Dyatlov, cut their way out of their tent on the slopes of Kholat Syakhl in the northern Urals and fled, poorly dressed, into temperatures as low as −40 °C. Six died of hypothermia; three had severe trauma (skull and chest injuries without matching external wounds), and some bodies found in a creek in May lacked eyes or a tongue. The Soviet inquest concluded a "compelling natural force" had killed them and sealed the files. Russian prosecutors reopened the case in 2019 and in 2020 concluded that an avalanche most likely forced them out of the tent.',
 [('Slab avalanche','Gaume and Puzrin (2021) modelled how a small slab avalanche, triggered by cutting the slope for the tent and by wind-blown snow, could cause the injuries; critics note that the site showed no obvious signs of an avalanche, that more than 100 later expeditions reported no avalanche conditions, and that terrain analyses judge the spot unlikely for one.'),
  ('Other natural causes','Katabatic winds, infrasound-induced panic, or ball lightning.'),
  ('Human or military causes','Speculation has included an attack by Mansi reindeer herders (rejected by the original investigation), fugitives, a botched spy meeting, or military rocket or nuclear tests (some radiation was found on one victim\'s clothing).'),
  ('UFO and paranormal theories','Another hiking group about 50 km south reported strange orange spheres in the sky that night, and similar spheres were reported in the area in February–March 1959 (witnesses came forward years later); these feed UFO theories, though nothing directly links them to the deaths.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s43247-020-00081-8','wiki:Dyatlov_Pass_incident'],'Dyatlov_Pass_incident',
 dateNote='1–2 February 1959'),
X('chelyabinsk','The Chelyabinsk meteor',H,'confirmed',2,2013,['scientific','video','infrasound'],
 'On the morning of 15 February 2013 an undetected near-Earth asteroid about 18 m across and about 9,100 tonnes entered the atmosphere over the southern Urals at about 19 km/s and exploded at around 30 km altitude above Chelyabinsk Oblast. The flash was briefly brighter than the Sun and some witnesses felt its heat. The airburst released energy equivalent to 400–500 kilotonnes of TNT, about 30 times the Hiroshima bomb, measured by infrasound stations built to detect nuclear tests. The shock wave damaged about 7,200 buildings and injured 1,491 people, mostly from flying glass; no one died. A fragment punched a hole in frozen Lake Chebarkul. It was the largest object to enter the atmosphere since Tunguska in 1908.',
 [('Coincidence with 2012 DA14','It arrived on the same day as the predicted close pass of asteroid 2012 DA14 but on a different orbit; scientists regard the timing as coincidence.'),
  ('Planetary defence','It showed that objects approaching from the Sun\'s direction can arrive unseen, spurring calls for better surveys and an international warning network.'),
  ('Ancient parallels (interpretation)','Its video record of a fireball, blast and falling stones is sometimes compared with ancient accounts of fire from the sky and with the disputed Younger Dryas impact hypothesis.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.1242642','wiki:Chelyabinsk_meteor'],'Chelyabinsk_meteor',
 dateNote='15 February 2013'),
]
